translation_en:
 His brilliance illuminates the Four Seas, yet does not overlook the minute and small.

Second Session

Discourse:
How a ruler sits with hands folded in solemnity and governs through non-action.

Imperial Edicts, Mandates, and Memorials [candidates are examined on one inside]:
Imitation of a Han-dynasty Imperial Edict deliberating on measures that can assist the common people [first year of the Later Era of Emperor Wen, 163 BCE];
Imitation of a Tang-dynasty Mandate appointing Lu Zhi 陸贄 as Jointly Manager of Affairs with the Secretariat-Chancellery [eighth year of the Zhenyuan era, 792 CE];
Imitation of a Memorial of Thanks from the Grand Secretaries for having received an Imperial Edict to respectfully compose the "Admonition for the Hall of Harmonious Reverence" (Yongsu dian zhen 雝肅殿箴), for the command to have it inscribed upon the Imperial Screen, and for the further bestowal of silver and silk.

Legal Judgments [five items]:
Officials exceeding the time limit in proceeding to their posts;
Mutual surveillance and detection regarding tax revenues and grain;
Performing sacrifices to deities registered in the sacrificial canon;
Frontier authorities requisitioning military supplies; …
